WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:21.293
morning Mayor Madeleine was at work when his secretary announced that Javert wanted to see him.

2
00:00:22.054 --> 00:00:23.014
Madeleine frowned.

3
00:00:23.855 --> 00:00:28.118
He had not seen the disagreeable policeman since their confrontation over Fantine.

4
00:00:28.138 --> 00:00:31.060
"Tell him to come in," said the mayor.

5
00:00:32.028 --> 00:00:33.349
Javert entered the room.

6
00:00:33.789 --> 00:00:37.210
The mayor remained seated at his desk, making notes on some papers.

7
00:00:38.231 --> 00:00:42.573
At last Madeleine looked up and said, "Well, Javert, what do you want?"

8
00:00:43.573 --> 00:00:47.835
Javert stayed silent for a moment longer with a sad expression on his face.

9
00:00:48.915 --> 00:00:51.976
Then he said, "A criminal act has been committed, Monsieur Mayor.

10
00:00:52.617 --> 00:00:56.038
A lowly government agent has committed a crime against a magistrate."

11
00:00:57.099 --> 00:00:59.179
"Really? Who is this government agent?"

12
00:00:59.199 --> 00:01:00.220
"I am."

13
00:01:00.636 --> 00:01:01.837
Javert lowered his eyes.

14
00:01:01.857 --> 00:01:05.138
"You? And who is this magistrate?"

15
00:01:05.158 --> 00:01:08.639
"You." Javert continued to look at the floor.

16
00:01:08.659 --> 00:01:10.860
"I've come to ask you to fire me.

17
00:01:12.441 --> 00:01:15.903
You may argue that I should simply resign, but that's not punishment enough for what I've done."

18
00:01:16.623 --> 00:01:17.823
Madeleine looked puzzled.

19
00:01:17.843 --> 00:01:19.564
"What's all this nonsense?

20
00:01:19.944 --> 00:01:21.765
What crime have you committed against me?"

21
00:01:21.785 --> 00:01:27.627
"Several weeks ago, after that episode in the police station with Fantine, I reported you to the chief

22
00:01:27.687 --> 00:01:28.608
of police in Paris."

23
00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:32.362
The mayor, who did not laugh often, began to laugh now.

24
00:01:32.382 --> 00:01:38.865
"Why? Because you felt I overstepped my authority as mayor?"

25
00:01:38.885 --> 00:01:42.768
"No, I reported you for being a former convict."

26
00:01:43.508 --> 00:01:45.589
Madeleine sat up straight in his chair.

27
00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:49.431
Javert, who still had not raised his eyes, continued.

28
00:01:49.451 --> 00:01:54.534
"For a long time I'd been suspicious because you reminded me of a convict from my days as

29
00:01:54.594 --> 00:01:55.695
a prison ship guard.

30
00:01:56.475 --> 00:01:59.961
You look like him, and you're incredibly strong just as he was.

31
00:02:00.601 --> 00:02:03.322
I mistook you for a man named Jean Valjean."

32
00:02:04.702 --> 00:02:05.662
The mayor started.

33
00:02:05.682 --> 00:02:09.063
"Who?" "A convict named Jean Valjean.

34
00:02:09.603 --> 00:02:13.565
Apparently after he was released, he robbed a bishop's home and then used a weapon to rob a

35
00:02:13.645 --> 00:02:15.405
chimney sweep along a deserted road.

36
00:02:16.165 --> 00:02:20.046
For years his whereabouts have been unknown, although the police have been looking for him.

37
00:02:21.007 --> 00:02:24.948
I was angry at you after you let Fantine go, so I wrote to the Paris police."

38
00:02:25.844 --> 00:02:28.847
Madeleine had gathered up his papers again and was busy reading them.

39
00:02:29.747 --> 00:02:35.012
Javert's facts were not quite right, but the policeman's words were still cause for alarm.

40
00:02:35.032 --> 00:02:37.454
"And what answer did you get from the police?"

41
00:02:37.934 --> 00:02:38.715
Madeleine asked.

42
00:02:38.735 --> 00:02:40.216
"That I was crazy.

43
00:02:40.776 --> 00:02:43.899
And I guess I must be because the real Jean Valjean has been found."

44
00:02:44.759 --> 00:02:48.823
The papers fell from the mayor's hand, and he looked steadily at Javert.

45
00:02:48.843 --> 00:02:52.266
"Ah," he said with no emotion in his voice.

46
00:02:52.886 --> 00:02:55.088
"Let me tell you what happened," said Javert.

47
00:02:56.468 --> 00:03:01.271
He explained that out in the country there lived a simple old man named Champmathieu.

48
00:03:02.151 --> 00:03:06.694
He was very poor, and no one paid much attention to him or how he managed to live.

49
00:03:07.594 --> 00:03:10.015
But he was recently arrested for stealing apples.

50
00:03:10.616 --> 00:03:13.757
He even had a branch of the apple tree in his hand when he was caught.

51
00:03:14.658 --> 00:03:16.159
The story should have ended there.

52
00:03:16.639 --> 00:03:20.801
But the jail was in need of repair, so he was taken to a prison in Arras.

53
00:03:21.682 --> 00:03:25.544
There, a convict named Brevet recognized him as Valjean.

54
00:03:26.224 --> 00:03:30.086
Since Valjean was such a notorious criminal, the police wanted to be sure.

55
00:03:30.606 --> 00:03:32.987
So they brought two more convicts to Arras.

56
00:03:33.987 --> 00:03:39.170
Their names were Cochepaille and Chenildieu, and they were both serving life sentences.

57
00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:41.871
Both of them recognized Valjean too.

58
00:03:41.891 --> 00:03:44.312
"Really," said the mayor.

59
00:03:44.332 --> 00:03:47.813
"Yes, and I also recognized him when I went to Arras.

60
00:03:48.754 --> 00:03:52.315
Now that I've seen him, I don't know how I could have mistaken you for him.

61
00:03:52.815 --> 00:03:54.076
I beg your pardon, Monsieur Mayor."

62
00:03:55.648 --> 00:03:59.471
"But what did this man say when he was accused of being Valjean?"

63
00:03:59.491 --> 00:04:05.096
"Anyone else would have protested violently, but he pretended he didn't understand what was going on.

64
00:04:05.937 --> 00:04:07.498
Oh, he's a clever rascal.

65
00:04:08.078 --> 00:04:10.120
It's a good thing he'll be going away for life."

66
00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:11.762
"For life?"

67
00:04:12.242 --> 00:04:12.802
said the mayor.

68
00:04:12.822 --> 00:04:13.843
"Oh, yes.

69
00:04:14.444 --> 00:04:19.788
Stealing apples is usually only a minor crime, but for a convict, it's a major crime punishable by

70
00:04:19.828 --> 00:04:21.510
a life sentence on the prison ships.

71
00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:24.112
I'm going to Arras to give my testimony."

72
00:04:24.928 --> 00:04:27.890
"All these details are of little interest to me," said Madeleine.

73
00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:30.511
"We both have many other things to do.

74
00:04:31.392 --> 00:04:34.333
I have at least four or five complaints here that I'd like you to investigate.

75
00:04:34.733 --> 00:04:36.214
When did you say you're giving testimony?"

76
00:04:36.234 --> 00:04:38.956
"I'm leaving tonight because the trial is tomorrow.

77
00:04:39.236 --> 00:04:42.858
The judge will decide the case by tomorrow night, but I won't wait around for that.

78
00:04:43.778 --> 00:04:45.419
I know Valjean will be convicted."

79
00:04:45.439 --> 00:04:47.200
"Then go."

80
00:04:47.601 --> 00:04:50.342
Mayor Madeleine dismissed Javert with a wave of his hand.

81
00:04:50.362 --> 00:04:53.564
"But you haven't fired me," Javert protested.

82
00:04:54.016 --> 00:04:55.337
"I deserve to lose my job.

83
00:04:56.077 --> 00:04:58.338
I've often been harsh on others when they have done wrong.

84
00:04:58.659 --> 00:05:00.980
So it's only right that I should be treated harshly too."

85
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.541
"We'll see about that."

86
00:05:03.001 --> 00:05:04.842
The mayor extended his hand to Javert.

87
00:05:04.862 --> 00:05:08.684
"A mayor shouldn't shake hands with a spy because that's all I am."

88
00:05:09.104 --> 00:05:11.826
Javert bowed and then he left the mayor's office.

89
00:05:12.826 --> 00:05:17.889
At first Madeleine thought that he should rush to Arras and save the prisoner named Champmathieu.

90
00:05:18.669 --> 00:05:21.951
He made arrangements for a carriage to pick him up early the next morning.

91
00:05:22.551 --> 00:05:25.332
But when he arrived home, he had second thoughts.

92
00:05:26.253 --> 00:05:31.454
He had always feared that someday he would hear that dreaded name, Jean Valjean, again.

93
00:05:32.555 --> 00:05:33.715
And now it had happened.

94
00:05:34.335 --> 00:05:38.516
Madeleine stayed up for most of the night, agonizing over what he should do.

95
00:05:38.536 --> 00:05:46.319
"Javert, who has been suspicious of me for so long, figured things out," the mayor said as he

96
00:05:46.359 --> 00:05:47.279
paced by the fire.

97
00:05:47.299 --> 00:05:50.720
"But this terrible bloodhound is off the scent now.

98
00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:56.604
He's satisfied that he knows who the real Valjean is, so he won't bother me anymore.

99
00:05:57.264 --> 00:05:58.365
The problem is solved."

100
00:05:59.185 --> 00:06:02.428
But Madeleine did not feel happy, so he thought some more.

101
00:06:03.068 --> 00:06:06.110
Perhaps if he admitted that he was Valjean, he would be pardoned.

102
00:06:06.951 --> 00:06:09.873
After all, he had lived a virtuous life ever since he met the bishop.

103
00:06:10.533 --> 00:06:13.816
Suddenly he felt as if the bishop were there with him, guiding him.

104
00:06:13.836 --> 00:06:20.568
"I'll do the right thing and save poor Champmathieu," vowed Madeleine.

105
00:06:20.608 --> 00:06:23.249
Then he remembered the coin he'd stolen from Little Gervais.

106
00:06:23.809 --> 00:06:26.450
That offense would condemn him to hard labor for life.

107
00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:28.851
And then he thought of Fantine.

108
00:06:29.491 --> 00:06:31.531
What would become of her if he went to prison?

109
00:06:32.232 --> 00:06:35.713
What kind of life would her unfortunate daughter have with the Thenardiers?

110
00:06:37.313 --> 00:06:38.794
Other people would be affected too.

111
00:06:38.814 --> 00:06:42.815
"My factory will go to ruin, and my employees will be out of work.

112
00:06:44.035 --> 00:06:46.656
The whole region will suffer if I am sent to prison again."

113
00:06:47.584 --> 00:06:50.346
For five hours the mayor paced in front of the fire.

114
00:06:50.866 --> 00:06:54.769
Finally exhausted, he dropped into his chair and fell asleep.

115
00:06:54.789 --> 00:06:56.470
"Monsieur Mayor!"

116
00:06:56.810 --> 00:06:58.892
called his housekeeper as she rapped on the door.

117
00:06:59.892 --> 00:07:00.813
He struggled awake.

118
00:07:01.393 --> 00:07:02.094
"What do you want?"

119
00:07:02.694 --> 00:07:04.696
"It's five o'clock and your carriage is here."

120
00:07:04.716 --> 00:07:06.257
"What carriage?"

121
00:07:06.837 --> 00:07:09.679
"The driver said you ordered a carriage to take you to Arras."

122
00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:14.902
There was a long silence while the mayor stood up and ran his fingers through his hair.

123
00:07:15.563 --> 00:07:16.884
He knew what he had to do.

124
00:07:17.768 --> 00:07:19.130
"What shall I say to the driver?"

125
00:07:19.551 --> 00:07:20.472
asked the housekeeper.

126
00:07:20.492 --> 00:07:22.555
"Tell him I'll be ready in a few minutes."
